{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`.bridge_state/bindings.json` keeps two indexes that must stay in lock-step: forward\n`bindings[local_id] = {jira_key, …}` and reverse `reverse[jira_key] = local_id`. A `reverse`\nentry that survives removal of its forward entry is reported by `rebar bridge fsck` forever as\n`store_integrity` / kind `reverse_missing_forward`, and **no supported surface can clean it\nup** — so the binding-drift canary alerts indefinitely on a benign fault, masking any genuine\nfuture integrity problem behind a constant non-zero count.\n\nAlready happened: 13 orphaned reverse keys (REB-410…REB-422) sat in the store until repaired\nunder nonliteral-spangly-fly (now closed), where the repair had to reach past the public API\ninto `BindingStore._data[\"reverse\"]` because nothing else could express it.\n\n## The defect (verified on current origin/main @ cc69cc1179)\n\n`BindingStore.unbind()` — `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py:426-430`:\n\n    def unbind(self, local_id: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Remove binding (for cleanup/rollback).\"\"\"\n        entry = self._data[\"bindings\"].pop(local_id, None)\n        if entry is not None and entry.get(\"jira_key\"):\n            self._data[\"reverse\"].pop(entry[\"jira_key\"], None)\n\nIt unconditionally pops the forward entry but pops the reverse entry **only if the forward\nentry still carries a `jira_key`** — cleanup of index B made conditional on index A, which it\njust destroyed. `_retire()` (`:591-592`) is the correct model: it pops both unconditionally.\n\n**Reachability, measured not assumed.** In *current* code the guard happens to hold: `reverse`\nis written only where a `jira_key` exists (`:422` `bind_confirm`, `:564-565`\n`note_absent_or_rekey`), so `reverse[K] = L` implies `bindings[L].jira_key == K`. `unbind()` is\n**incidentally** safe today — safe by a whole-file invariant, not by its own logic.\n\n**The already-realised hole:** only `unbind()` (`:428`) and `_retire()` (`:592`) remove a\nforward entry. Any *other* route leaks the reverse key permanently — an out-of-band prune, a\nmanual edit, or a merge artifact. The tickets branch resolves `bindings.json` as `merge=ours`\n(`docs/concurrency.md:414`), so a concurrent divergence can drop forward entries wholesale\nwith no reverse pop. That produced the 13 repaired keys.\n\n## Approach\n\nTwo independent changes; either alone is an improvement, both close the hole.\n\n**1. Make `unbind()` self-contained** (`binding_store.py:426-430`). Keep the O(1) keyed pop as\na fast path, then sweep the reverse index for any residual key still pointing at `local_id`, so\nthe method is correct regardless of the forward entry's contents:\n\n    entry = self._data[\"bindings\"].pop(local_id, None)\n    reverse = self._data[\"reverse\"]\n    if entry is not None and entry.get(\"jira_key\"):\n        reverse.pop(entry[\"jira_key\"], None)\n    for key in [k for k, v in reverse.items() if v == local_id]:\n        reverse.pop(key, None)\n\nThe leverage: after this, `unbind(L)` also clears a *stranded* reverse key — the forward pop\nno-ops and the sweep removes every `reverse[K] == L`. So fix 1 makes the orphan class\nexpressible through the existing public API and fix 2 needs **no new `BindingStore` method** —\nwhich matters, as `binding_store.py` is 785/800 LOC.\n\n**2. Add a supported prune verb.** New module `src/rebar/_commands/bridge_repair.py` holding\n`prune_orphan_reverse_bindings(tracker_dir, *, force_reason) -> int`. `bridge_fsck.main()`\n(`bridge_fsck.py:590`, argparse at `:606-618`) gains a `--repair` flag that delegates to it —\nthe audit functions stay pure, preserving the L9 audit boundary (`bridge_fsck.py:199,342`).\n`rebar bridge fsck` already forwards REMAINDER args to `main()`\n(`_cli/_bridge_commands.py:117-122,201-202`), so no new CLI plumbing; only\n`_bridge_commands.py:59` (metavar) and `:216-220` (help text) mention the flag.\n\nDeletion runs as `store.unbind(local_id)` per audited orphan, then one `store.save()` (atomic\ntempfile + `os.replace`, `binding_store.py:292`) — never a raw rewrite, never a `_data` reach.\n\nThe verb takes its finding set from the existing `audit_store_integrity()`\n(`bridge_fsck.py:198`) rather than re-deriving orphans — the repair-consumes-the-detector\ndiscipline of `tracker_maintenance.py:93` — and applies four guards, each aborting before any\nwrite:\n\n1. every `store_integrity` finding must be kind `reverse_missing_forward` — refuse if another\n   kind is present (never repair a store carrying an unaudited fault);\n2. no orphan key may appear in `bindings-retired.json` (retired means tombstone, not delete);\n3. no orphan key may have a forward binding under any spelling;\n4. post-deletion the forward map must be unchanged and no orphan may remain.\n\nA durable audit line follows the `tracker_maintenance.py:100-118` model — one `json.dumps`\nline in the tracker's **git dir**, not the worktree, so it never becomes merge-conflicting\nstore content — recording timestamp, actor, argv, deleted keys, and before/after counts.\n\n**Exit codes:** `0` healed or nothing to do, `1` a guard refused, `2` operational failure.\n\n## Scope\n\nSee the recorded `file_impact` for the exact path set.\n\n**Out of scope (deliberate, minimal surface):** exposing the prune through the library facade\nor MCP — it is an operator break-glass, not an agent-callable write. Follow-up if wanted.\n\n## Testing\n\nFixtures follow the existing local helpers — `BindingStore(tmp_path / \".tickets-tracker\")` +\n`bind_confirm` (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_binding_walk.py:58-63`) and the raw-JSON\ntracker builders in `tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_fsck_binding_drift.py`.\n\n- **RED (pins fix 1):** store with `reverse[K] = L` where `bindings[L]` exists but carries no\n  `jira_key`; `unbind(L)`; assert `K` is gone from `reverse`. Fails today.\n- **Regression:** `unbind()` on a normally-confirmed binding still removes both indexes.\n- **Prune:** happy path, refusal on a foreign integrity kind, refusal on a retired orphan key,\n  and no-op on a healthy store — one test each, as enumerated in the criteria below.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `unbind()` removes the reverse entry without reading the forward entry's `jira_key`.\n      Verified by the RED test: it fails on `cc69cc1179` and passes after the change.\n- [ ] `unbind()` on a normally-confirmed binding still clears both indexes. Verified by the\n      regression test above.\n- [ ] `rebar bridge fsck --repair` reduces `store_integrity` from N to 0 on a store seeded\n      with N `reverse_missing_forward` orphans. Verified by the prune happy-path test.\n- [ ] The prune mutates no forward entry: `bindings` is byte-identical before and after.\n      Verified by comparing the serialised forward map in the happy-path test.\n- [ ] The prune leaves `bindings-retired.json` byte-identical. Verified by a sha256 compare\n      in the happy-path test.\n- [ ] The prune exits 1 and writes nothing when any non-`reverse_missing_forward`\n      `store_integrity` kind is present. Verified by the refusal test.\n- [ ] The prune exits 1 and writes nothing when an orphan `jira_key` is present in\n      `bindings-retired.json`. Verified by the retired-guard test.\n- [ ] The prune exits 0 and mutates no file on a healthy store. Verified by the no-op test\n      asserting the `bindings.json` mtime and bytes are unchanged.\n- [ ] The prune performs deletion via `BindingStore.unbind()` + `save()`. Verified by\n      `grep -n \"_data\" src/rebar/_commands/bridge_repair.py` returning no match.\n- [ ] `bridge_fsck.py`'s audit functions are unchanged apart from `main()`'s flag dispatch.\n      Verified by `git diff` touching only `main()` in that file.\n- [ ] `make lint` passes.\n- [ ] `make typecheck` passes.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/scripts/bridge tests/unit/rebar_reconciler` passes.\n- [ ] No `src/rebar` file exceeds 800 LOC (the CI module-size gate)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786558490794741001,"uuid":"a28d0de2-0b29-484a-8122-51406b43a4c5"}